1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
To think, to reason, makes us human, sets us apart from the rest of nature.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
People communicate in intricate ways about complex subjects,

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and at the center of it all, a thinking machine, the computer.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
With it, our thoughts can be recorded.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
What once seemed impossible, now seems commonplace.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Today, computers are capable of amazing tasks that boggle the mind.

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:37,200
But the evolution of a thinking machine, a computer, engaged some of the greatest minds of the last two centuries.

8
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:47,920
The story of the computer contains squandered chances, fortunate accidents, frequent missteps, fantastic insights, and unprecedented genius.

9
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:58,280
It is the story of a machine that helps us win a war, figures in the creation of weapons of inconceivable destruction, and sends us into space.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,649
The birth of the computer spawned the growth of whole industries, led to the world's greatest personal fortune, and will increasingly affect the lives of everyone on

11
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:09,350
Earth.

12
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:16,460
Coming up on Modern Marvels, Thinking Machines, the creation of the computer.

13
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Our world is increasingly filled with countless wonders

14
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:47,840
that would not have been possible without one machine, the computer.

15
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:50,780
Although computers are enormously intricate,

16
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,980
their most basic components consist of simple devices

17
00:01:53,981 --> 00:01:58,020
that can be switched to either one of two states, on or off.

18
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,920
The computer creates its magic by calculating with a speed and accuracy that far surpasses its human inventors.

19
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,220
Computers stagger the mind with their complexity.

20
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:14,900
But simply put, a computer takes information, processes it, and then outputs a result.

21
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:19,720
It's all done with a unique partnership of hardware and software.

22
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,140
Hardware comes in boxes.

23
00:02:22,141 --> 00:02:25,920
They're the physical components, such as the monitor and hard drive.

24
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:28,320
Software comes on disks.

25
00:02:28,321 --> 00:02:32,320
Software consists of instructions that tell the computer what to do.

26
00:02:32,321 --> 00:02:38,320
One way to start this computational partnership is to type on the keyboard, providing input.

27
00:02:38,321 --> 00:02:44,320
The input is picked up by the central processing unit, or CPU, the computer's brain.

28
00:02:44,321 --> 00:02:49,320
Using instructions provided by software, the CPU processes the input.

29
00:02:49,321 --> 00:02:53,320
The magic behind the CPU is its blinding speed.

30
00:02:53,321 --> 00:02:58,200
Modern processors are measured in MIPS, millions of instructions per second.

31
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:07,020
While processing, the CPU may retrieve data stored in random access memory known as RAM, or data stored on a hard drive.

32
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:14,040
Modern RAM is so quick that every second it can send the equivalent of 10,000 typewritten pages of information to the CPU.

33
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:20,860
And modern hard drives can store the equivalent of 250,000 pages of typewritten material.

34
00:03:20,861 --> 00:03:25,860
After processing, the CPU outputs information, often on a monitor.

35
00:03:25,861 --> 00:03:30,860
The whole procedure is usually so quick that it appears instantaneous.

36
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:35,860
Today, computers are so commonplace we take them for granted.

37
00:03:35,861 --> 00:03:40,860
But not long ago, computers only existed in the imagination of a few visionaries.

38
00:03:40,861 --> 00:03:45,860
The search for machines that could figure quickly and accurately

39
00:03:45,861 --> 00:03:49,860
has seized the human imagination for thousands of years.

40
00:03:49,861 --> 00:03:53,860
In fact, the computer's family tree has roots so deep in the past,

41
00:03:53,861 --> 00:03:56,860
it's impossible to know exactly where they begin.

42
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:10,860
By the early 19th century, the European Industrial Revolution was well underway.

43
00:04:10,861 --> 00:04:15,860
And the development in production and commerce came from the maturing fields of engineering,

44
00:04:15,861 --> 00:04:19,860
navigation, surveying, finance, and science.

45
00:04:19,861 --> 00:04:25,860
The practical application of these fields relied on volume after volume of tables.

46
00:04:25,861 --> 00:04:30,860
Tables for trigonometry, tides, interest rates, multiplication, and gravity.

47
00:04:30,861 --> 00:04:32,860
Tables were critical.

48
00:04:32,861 --> 00:04:37,860
The actual figuring was done by people who specialized in mathematical computation.

49
00:04:37,861 --> 00:04:40,860
Surprisingly, these people had a familiar job title.

50
00:04:40,861 --> 00:04:42,860
They were called computers.

51
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,020
These human computers toiled over their tables incessantly,

52
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:49,520
monotonously, and made mistakes.

53
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:52,380
Typically, tables were full of errors.

54
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:55,980
The requirement for accurate tables

55
00:04:55,981 --> 00:04:58,880
introduced one of the most eccentric and brilliant figures

56
00:04:58,881 --> 00:05:02,500
into the story of computers, Charles Babbage.

57
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,220
Oh, Babbage was an extraordinary scientist.

58
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:07,600
I mean, Babbage was a great scientist.

59
00:05:07,601 --> 00:05:10,360
Babbage was 100 years ahead of his time.

60
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,220
You can't say that about many people,

61
00:05:12,221 --> 00:05:13,680
but you could say that about Babbage.

62
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:18,780
Charles Babbage represented that extraordinary element of British society,

63
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:20,580
the scientist-aristocrat.

64
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:25,340
Many were known for their eccentricities, and Babbage was no exception.

65
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:29,380
As a youth, Babbage devised footwear of hinged boards

66
00:05:29,381 --> 00:05:31,720
intended to allow him to walk on water.

67
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:35,280
Never one to shirk adventure, he tried them out himself,

68
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:37,220
but flipped over and nearly drowned.

69
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:40,540
Babbage demonstrated his brilliance in mathematics

70
00:05:40,541 --> 00:05:42,640
while attending Trinity College in Cambridge.

71
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:47,220
In 1820, Babbage was checking the accuracy of calculations

72
00:05:47,221 --> 00:05:51,280
made for the Royal Astronomical Society and kept finding errors.

73
00:05:52,340 --> 00:05:54,560
He reasoned that a machine could be constructed

74
00:05:54,561 --> 00:05:57,760
that would calculate the tables and directly print the results.

75
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:01,300
He called the machine the difference engine.

76
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:04,300
He drew up plans for a section of the device

77
00:06:04,301 --> 00:06:07,260
and had it built with his own funds in 1822.

78
00:06:07,261 --> 00:06:12,640
Babbage couldn't pay for the construction of the entire device, but since the greatest

79
00:06:12,641 --> 00:06:16,920
beneficiaries would be the British government and people, he made the extraordinary step

80
00:06:16,921 --> 00:06:20,740
of petitioning the government for a grant.

81
00:06:20,741 --> 00:06:26,880
In 1823, the Treasury provided the project with start-up funds.

82
00:06:26,881 --> 00:06:29,480
Government support for the computer industry is nothing new.

83
00:06:29,481 --> 00:06:34,940
It's very much a big topic in the news today, and it will continue to be.

84
00:06:34,941 --> 00:06:40,360
is an expensive proposition, and it usually requires some government support if it's going

85
00:06:40,361 --> 00:06:41,478
to get anywhere.

86
00:06:41,479 --> 00:06:46,300
Babbage hired a mechanical engineer, set to work on a complete design

87
00:06:46,301 --> 00:06:50,394
for the difference engine, and immediately ran into difficulties.

88
00:06:50,395 --> 00:06:51,780
The mechanical machine

89
00:06:51,781 --> 00:06:56,040
shops of the time were not advanced enough to produce parts in the precise measurements

90
00:06:56,041 --> 00:06:57,853
that Babbage's plans required.

91
00:06:57,854 --> 00:07:01,720
So Babbage designed better machine tools, which would eventually

92
00:07:01,721 --> 00:07:06,120
improved the entire state of British tool manufacturing.

93
00:07:06,121 --> 00:07:11,700
By 1829, Babbage had spent the 1,500 pound grant from the government and even more than

94
00:07:11,701 --> 00:07:14,260
that from his own funds.

95
00:07:14,261 --> 00:07:19,140
But only a few bits and pieces of machine had been completed.

96
00:07:19,141 --> 00:07:23,000
Babbage's project began to attract critics.

97
00:07:23,001 --> 00:07:25,600
He was plagued by several problems.

98
00:07:25,601 --> 00:07:30,960
One of his problems being his perfectionism, another problem being that his work was not

99
00:07:30,961 --> 00:07:35,900
was not understood or appreciated by the people of his time.

100
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:38,780
Babbage had many enemies.

101
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:41,440
Even London's organ grinders despised him

102
00:07:41,441 --> 00:07:43,140
because he had tried to have them banned

103
00:07:43,141 --> 00:07:44,980
as their music interfered with his thinking.

104
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,760
But the inventor continued to toil,

105
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:50,780
and finally, in 1832,

106
00:07:51,180 --> 00:07:53,800
there were enough parts to assemble a section of the engine.

107
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:55,700
It functioned perfectly,

108
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:59,060
solving equations and producing six-digit results.

109
00:07:59,061 --> 00:08:03,160
But it was only a small part of the proposed machine.

110
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:10,080
The skyrocketing costs and lack of results finally made the government pull its support from the project.

111
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:16,980
Although disappointed by the cancellation, Babbage had contributed to the project's demise

112
00:08:16,981 --> 00:08:24,360
by suggesting that a new device he had conceived, the analytical engine, would be vastly superior to his old design.

113
00:08:24,361 --> 00:08:32,760
Babbage, in hindsight, probably should have finished the difference engine and seen how

114
00:08:32,761 --> 00:08:37,360
far he could have gone with that before starting the analytical engine.

115
00:08:37,361 --> 00:08:43,360
There's no question that the analytical engine was more than he could handle.

116
00:08:43,361 --> 00:08:45,660
Babbage was obsessed with his new idea.

117
00:08:45,661 --> 00:08:49,480
With the analytical engine, Babbage asked himself, why not build a machine that could

118
00:08:49,481 --> 00:08:52,940
solve any mathematical problem?

119
00:08:52,941 --> 00:08:58,240
At the age of 43, Babbage had the vision of a computer, a vision he pursued for the rest

120
00:08:58,241 --> 00:09:00,320
of his life.

121
00:09:00,321 --> 00:09:05,520
The extraordinary fact is that Babbage's overall design for the analytical engine had many

122
00:09:05,521 --> 00:09:10,100
components analogous to those in a modern computer.

123
00:09:10,101 --> 00:09:14,820
The heart of the machine, the mill, made the calculations, like the central processing

124
00:09:14,821 --> 00:09:17,740
unit of modern computers.

125
00:09:17,741 --> 00:09:23,560
An oblong structure, the store, held numbers to be used in the calculations, like modern

126
00:09:23,561 --> 00:09:26,800
computer memory.

127
00:09:26,801 --> 00:09:31,760
Instructions and numbers could be fed into the machine using punch cards.

128
00:09:31,761 --> 00:09:35,660
Much of what we know about the workings of the analytical engine came from the writings

129
00:09:35,661 --> 00:09:39,060
of Ada, Countess of Lovelace.

130
00:09:39,061 --> 00:09:46,220
Among the people who understood what Babbage was doing was a woman named Ada Augusta, who

131
00:09:46,221 --> 00:09:50,500
who was the daughter of, one of the daughters of Lord Byron, the poet.

132
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:56,400
She had studied mathematics as a child and had quite a bit of talent.

133
00:09:57,360 --> 00:09:59,940
Ada met Babbage at one of his famous dinner parties

134
00:09:59,941 --> 00:10:03,500
that were often attended by the luminaries of British science and engineering.

135
00:10:04,740 --> 00:10:08,200
Babbage demonstrated the working section of the difference engine for her,

136
00:10:08,201 --> 00:10:10,720
and she was immediately captivated by it.

137
00:10:10,721 --> 00:10:17,720
She published a simple description of Babbage's vision for the analytical engine.

138
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:23,720
Ada wrote some descriptions of it and she also appended to these descriptions

139
00:10:23,721 --> 00:10:28,720
a hypothetical way that this machine could solve an equation.

140
00:10:28,721 --> 00:10:34,720
And on the basis of those descriptions, people often call her the world's first programmer.

141
00:10:34,721 --> 00:10:37,720
But she was never to program a real machine.

142
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:42,480
As Babbage entered the last years of his life, his great work was unfinished.

143
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:46,840
He had become cranky and suffered constant attack by his many enemies.

144
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:52,400
In 1871, when London's organ grinders discovered that Babbage was ill,

145
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:58,020
they surrounded his house and serenaded him, increasing his agony until he died.

146
00:10:59,580 --> 00:11:03,920
Only a small portion of the analytical engine was built in Babbage's lifetime.

147
00:11:03,921 --> 00:11:10,680
Babbage's vision of the computer fell into obscurity, and except for the detailed text

148
00:11:10,681 --> 00:11:13,320
left by Ada, could well have been forgotten.

149
00:11:13,321 --> 00:11:20,920
Bob Babbage's machines, which were never finished, which existed for the most part only on paper,

150
00:11:20,921 --> 00:11:27,506
were proto-computers. They were mechanical, they used gears, they used metal shafts.

151
00:11:27,507 --> 00:11:28,207
They

152
00:11:28,208 --> 00:11:32,160
weren't computers in our sense of the term, that is, they weren't electronic digital computers,

153
00:11:32,161 --> 00:11:36,660
but they were abstractly and on paper mechanical computers.

154
00:11:36,661 --> 00:11:43,660
It would be nearly 100 years before a programmable computation device would again be conceived.

155
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:57,660
In the late 1800s, the idea of a programmable computer was a distant dream.

156
00:11:57,661 --> 00:12:01,160
But the American dream was becoming a reality.

157
00:12:01,161 --> 00:12:07,940
America was on its way to becoming the world's greatest industrial power and opened its arms to immigrants from other lands.

158
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:15,560
In the second half of the 19th century, America's population increased 35% each decade.

159
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:23,740
America's exploding population began to endanger one of its great institutions, the American Census.

160
00:12:23,741 --> 00:12:33,020
The census, which is required by the Constitution to be held every ten years, was still being

161
00:12:33,021 --> 00:12:40,600
done by old-fashioned people making check marks on pieces of paper, and it simply couldn't

162
00:12:40,601 --> 00:12:46,700
keep up with the tremendous surge of population in the U.S.

163
00:12:46,701 --> 00:12:49,660
The crisis reached ahead in 1887.

164
00:12:49,661 --> 00:12:55,160
the Census Bureau was still hand-tallying the data from the 1880 census.

165
00:12:55,161 --> 00:13:02,220
Desperate for relief, the Bureau pleaded for any method that could speed up the counting of the 1890 census.

166
00:13:02,221 --> 00:13:09,060
The superintendent of the census had proposals for three systems, so he decided to stage a contest.

167
00:13:09,061 --> 00:13:12,120
Two of the systems relied on hand counting.

168
00:13:12,121 --> 00:13:18,600
The third, developed by a young, rather humorless, former MIT instructor named Herman Hollerith,

169
00:13:18,601 --> 00:13:20,840
used punched cards.

170
00:13:20,841 --> 00:13:23,580
Punch cards would one day become the standard method

171
00:13:23,581 --> 00:13:26,520
of feeding high volumes of data into computers.

172
00:13:27,660 --> 00:13:31,620
Now where he got this idea, we are not sure.

173
00:13:31,621 --> 00:13:35,860
He may have been inspired by the fact that a conductor

174
00:13:35,861 --> 00:13:40,620
on a railroad punches your ticket when you hand it to him.

175
00:13:40,621 --> 00:13:42,920
Polarith's system beat the others easily.

176
00:13:42,921 --> 00:13:44,820
In the tabulation portion of the test,

177
00:13:44,821 --> 00:13:47,720
it was nearly 10 times faster.

178
00:13:47,721 --> 00:13:55,280
The Census Bureau leased 56 of Hollerwitz's machines at $1,000 a year each and put them to work in July 1890.

179
00:13:56,800 --> 00:14:01,600
Census Bureau clerks used Hollerwitz's machines to punch the cards and then tabulate the results.

180
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:06,660
Scores of operators were trained to use the puncher quickly and accurately.

181
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:09,360
The tabulating was done with electricity.

182
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:14,640
A metal pin that passed through a card hole made electrical contact with a cup of mercury,

183
00:14:14,641 --> 00:14:21,640
completing a circuit that was registered on a tallying device that consisted of rows of clock-like dials.

184
00:14:21,641 --> 00:14:26,640
Hollerith's machines were a step toward the later development of computers.

185
00:14:26,641 --> 00:14:30,640
They significantly sped up the processing of information.

186
00:14:30,641 --> 00:14:34,640
The results of the 1890 census count were a triumph for Hollerith.

187
00:14:34,641 --> 00:14:43,640
In just six weeks, the population count of 62,622,250 was tallied.

188
00:14:43,641 --> 00:14:47,640
Hollerith became the talk of the scientific community.

189
00:14:47,641 --> 00:14:51,640
He rented an office and set himself up in business.

190
00:14:51,641 --> 00:14:55,640
He called his new enterprise the Tabulating Machine Company.

191
00:14:55,641 --> 00:14:59,640
Hollerith had the Census Bureau business in his pocket, and the future looked bright.

192
00:14:59,641 --> 00:15:03,640
But it turned out to be harder than it seemed.

193
00:15:33,640 --> 00:15:37,520
scattered all across the country.

194
00:15:37,521 --> 00:15:39,680
Hundreds of clerks produced tons of paper

195
00:15:39,681 --> 00:15:42,820
to help track and manage these vast empires.

196
00:15:42,821 --> 00:15:45,380
Hollerith convinced the New York Central Railroad

197
00:15:45,381 --> 00:15:48,580
to try out some of his machines.

198
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:50,980
The experiment wasn't a success.

199
00:15:50,981 --> 00:15:53,680
Hollerith's machines could compute fast enough for census

200
00:15:53,681 --> 00:15:56,380
work, but couldn't keep up with the speed and volume

201
00:15:56,381 --> 00:15:57,980
of the railroad business.

202
00:15:57,981 --> 00:16:01,520
After three months, the machines were removed.

203
00:16:01,521 --> 00:16:03,940
Hollerith was short on capital and faced ruin.

204
00:16:04,740 --> 00:16:06,980
He moved his family into his mother-in-law's house.

205
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:12,220
He sold his assets, even his horse, to raise money to redesign his machines,

206
00:16:12,221 --> 00:16:16,100
to improve their speed, reliability, and ability to make additions.

207
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:20,620
Hollerith even customized the punch cards for business computations,

208
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:23,600
such as adding columns to store dollars and cents.

209
00:16:24,540 --> 00:16:28,560
After a solid year of tedious work, Hollerith returned to the New York Central

210
00:16:28,561 --> 00:16:33,600
and offered them free use of his new, improved, and faster computing machines for a year.

211
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:39,760
Within three months, the railroad was convinced and contracted to lease the machines.

212
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:44,000
The tabulating machine company was back on track.

213
00:16:45,420 --> 00:16:48,940
Hollerith had avoided bankruptcy and now had more work than he could handle.

214
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:53,820
And Hollerith, you could say, came along just in time.

215
00:16:53,821 --> 00:16:58,320
It was a combination of his invention making this available.

216
00:16:58,560 --> 00:17:03,860
but also the need out there required something like that.

217
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:08,940
So it was a convergence of the social needs or the social factors on the one hand

218
00:17:08,941 --> 00:17:12,140
and the inventiveness sort of pushing from the other hand.

219
00:17:12,680 --> 00:17:13,920
But Hollerith was weary.

220
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:17,560
He was diagnosed with a bad heart in order to slow down.

221
00:17:18,380 --> 00:17:23,580
In 1911, Hollerith sold his shares in the tabulating machine company for over $1 million.

222
00:17:23,581 --> 00:17:24,281
dollars.

223
00:17:24,282 --> 00:17:30,660
Hollerith's former company was merged with three others and led by master salesman

224
00:17:30,661 --> 00:17:37,089
Thomas Watson, grew into a major supplier of business equipment.

225
00:17:37,090 --> 00:17:39,400
In 1924, Watson renamed

226
00:17:39,401 --> 00:17:43,526
the enterprise International Business Machines, IBM.

227
00:17:43,527 --> 00:17:46,700
Because of Hollerith, the name IBM would

228
00:17:46,701 --> 00:17:51,040
become synonymous with computers.

229
00:17:51,041 --> 00:17:54,860
By the 1930s, as America limped out of the Great Depression,

230
00:17:54,861 --> 00:17:57,540
companies like Burroughs and IBM foresaw

231
00:17:57,541 --> 00:18:02,460
continued growth and success.

232
00:18:02,461 --> 00:18:05,520
Over the next decade, progress would be slow.

233
00:18:05,521 --> 00:18:08,460
It would take the destructive forces of World War II

234
00:18:08,461 --> 00:18:11,460
to give the computer its next great advance.

235
00:18:16,700 --> 00:18:23,820
World War II spurred the development of the true computer.

236
00:18:24,540 --> 00:18:27,780
And in the turbulent days before the German Blitzkrieg smashed Poland,

237
00:18:27,781 --> 00:18:33,380
a young Polish engineer walked into the British Embassy in Warsaw and made an astounding proposition.

238
00:18:34,260 --> 00:18:39,880
He offered to sell the British the secret to the unbreakable German code machine, the Enigma.

239
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:44,820
The British desperately wanted to crack the Enigma machine,

240
00:18:44,821 --> 00:18:49,900
used by German commanders to encrypt their most secret military radio messages.

241
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:55,740
Radio is a wonderful piece of technology, but the very beauty of radio,

242
00:18:55,920 --> 00:19:01,340
the fact that you broadcast messages out, makes it very bad for military traffic

243
00:19:01,341 --> 00:19:06,320
because the enemy can read your messages as well as the person you intended to read.

244
00:19:06,321 --> 00:19:08,900
So you encrypt the message.

245
00:19:09,900 --> 00:19:13,440
British intelligence supplied the engineer with a fake diplomatic passport

246
00:19:13,441 --> 00:19:15,440
and smuggled him out of Warsaw.

247
00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:19,940
While guarded by French agents in Paris,

248
00:19:19,941 --> 00:19:24,440
the engineer provided details on the code machine's ingenious operation.

249
00:19:24,441 --> 00:19:30,440
In the Enigma, plugs were rearranged to conform to that day's codebook combination.

250
00:19:30,441 --> 00:19:33,440
The power of Enigma was that this plug arrangement

251
00:19:33,441 --> 00:19:38,440
constantly varied how letters were coded throughout a transmission.

252
00:19:38,441 --> 00:19:41,440
The number of letter variations was astronomical,

253
00:19:41,441 --> 00:19:45,600
so high the Germans considered their code machine to be unbreakable.

254
00:19:48,400 --> 00:19:51,240
But the British now knew how the machine worked.

255
00:19:51,720 --> 00:19:56,720
They realized that they could very quickly try different key combinations on a small part of the code.

256
00:19:57,360 --> 00:20:03,100
Then, when that small part was broken and the key revealed, the rest could be decoded effortlessly.

257
00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:08,560
North of London, at a secret installation in Bletchley Park,

258
00:20:08,561 --> 00:20:13,920
British code breakers built a computer-like machine to do just that, Colossus.

259
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:19,280
Colossus used over 2,000 vacuum tubes to process 25,000 characters per second.

260
00:20:20,100 --> 00:20:24,340
Colossus could only do one job, but it could compute very quickly.

261
00:20:24,980 --> 00:20:30,340
Its deciphered German transmissions were called the Ultra Secret, the most closely guarded secret of the war.

262
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:33,980
While the British were now able to read German messages,

263
00:20:33,981 --> 00:20:39,960
the question was how to take advantage of the secrets without tipping off the Germans that their code had been compromised.

264
00:20:41,120 --> 00:20:44,620
The most senior Allied commanders were privy to Ultra material,

265
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:49,420
but had to exercise caution in reacting to it so as not to tip their hand.

266
00:20:50,820 --> 00:20:55,560
Ultra information was never revealed to anyone in a position to be captured by the enemy.

267
00:20:56,480 --> 00:21:00,400
Field commanders often went into battle lacking information on the enemy

268
00:21:00,401 --> 00:21:03,720
that was known to their superiors from Ultra dispatches.

269
00:21:03,980 --> 00:21:10,980
This secrecy may have also kept Colossus from prominence in the history of computers.

270
00:21:13,980 --> 00:21:20,980
While Colossus was breaking German codes, across the Atlantic another computational device was under construction.

271
00:21:20,981 --> 00:21:28,980
The machine, which would directly influence the design of all future computers, was being built in Philadelphia.

272
00:21:33,980 --> 00:21:46,520
In response to the attack on Pearl Harbor, American industry quickly became a powerhouse

273
00:21:46,521 --> 00:21:50,900
producer of the implements of war.

274
00:21:50,901 --> 00:21:56,860
But by 1943, there was a critical shortage of a surprising component of the war machine.

275
00:21:56,861 --> 00:22:01,380
Firing tables were artillery pieces.

276
00:22:01,381 --> 00:22:06,620
tables allowed gunners to correctly aim their guns in different ranges, altitudes, temperatures,

277
00:22:06,621 --> 00:22:07,620
and wind conditions.

278
00:22:07,621 --> 00:22:14,200
To calculate these tables required enormous numbers of calculations, which at that time

279
00:22:14,201 --> 00:22:15,920
were done by human beings.

280
00:22:15,921 --> 00:22:20,020
Incidentally, these people who did them were called computers.

281
00:22:20,021 --> 00:22:21,654
That was their job title.

282
00:22:21,655 --> 00:22:25,640
And they operated adding machines, mechanical adding machines

283
00:22:25,641 --> 00:22:26,341
primarily.

284
00:22:26,342 --> 00:22:31,140
And they would simply step through these calculations and produce these tables.

285
00:22:31,141 --> 00:22:38,800
One of the centers of firing table calculation was at the Moore School of Engineering at the University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia.

286
00:22:39,900 --> 00:22:44,620
Midway through the war, it became clear that the tables could not be produced fast enough.

287
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:46,972
This was a crisis.

288
00:22:46,973 --> 00:22:51,080
Without tables, new guns could not be shipped to the troops overseas.

289
00:22:52,540 --> 00:22:58,760
To break the bottleneck, a Moore School physicist, John Moakley, made a fantastic proposal.

290
00:22:58,761 --> 00:23:07,760
He suggested that he could build a giant electronic computer that would be able to figure a single trajectory in 100 seconds.

291
00:23:07,761 --> 00:23:16,760
The Army, desperate for a device to help them win the war, reluctantly committed to the proposed cost of half a million dollars.

292
00:23:16,761 --> 00:23:27,760
Mockley and a brilliant graduate student in electrical engineering, Presper Eckert, set to work constructing ENIAC, the Electronic Numeric Integrator and Computer.

293
00:23:27,761 --> 00:23:34,760
Driven by the knowledge that friends and relatives were dying in battle while they worked in Philadelphia,

294
00:23:34,761 --> 00:23:37,760
the team of young engineers toiled incessantly.

295
00:23:37,761 --> 00:23:41,760
But could they create such a monster?

296
00:23:41,761 --> 00:23:45,760
Everything had to be invented from square one.

297
00:23:45,761 --> 00:23:48,760
And then they had to build it. And then they had to test it.

298
00:23:48,761 --> 00:23:51,760
And they had to put it all together and make it work reliably.

299
00:23:51,761 --> 00:23:54,760
And then they had to learn how to program it.

300
00:23:54,761 --> 00:23:58,937
Nothing close to ENIAC had ever been conceived.

301
00:23:58,938 --> 00:24:12,180
Nearly 100 feet long and weighing 30 tons, it contained almost 70,000 resistors, 10,000 capacitors, 6,000 switches, and 18,000 delicate vacuum tubes.

302
00:24:12,181 --> 00:24:16,180
Vacuum tubes burn out just like light bulbs.

303
00:24:16,181 --> 00:24:19,180
In a machine that contained 18,000 vacuum tubes,

304
00:24:19,181 --> 00:24:24,180
it was likely that at least one would always be burned out, crippling the machine.

305
00:24:24,181 --> 00:24:28,180
Presper Eckert found the key to making ENIAC function.

306
00:24:28,181 --> 00:24:31,180
He had the vacuum tubes built to high tolerances.

307
00:24:31,181 --> 00:24:35,180
He critically tested them, and then he ran them at low power.

308
00:24:35,181 --> 00:24:40,180
If you took these measures, you might be able to get the machine to work for 10 minutes,

309
00:24:40,181 --> 00:24:45,280
minutes half an hour at a time since the machine calculated so quickly you can

310
00:24:45,281 --> 00:24:50,560
get a lot of work done in half hour and so that's what happened after two years

311
00:24:50,561 --> 00:24:55,840
of intense work ENIAC was complete a few months after the Japanese surrender

312
00:24:55,841 --> 00:25:00,400
although it wasn't finished in time to help win the war ENIAC was a marvelous

313
00:25:00,401 --> 00:25:07,120
machine huge and hot it could perform up to 5,000 additions 357 multiplications

314
00:25:07,121 --> 00:25:09,360
and 38 divisions every second.

315
00:25:12,180 --> 00:25:15,520
By far and away the most complex machine of its time,

316
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:19,020
ENIAC still lacked many of the qualities of a modern computer.

317
00:25:19,580 --> 00:25:21,080
Its memory was very primitive.

318
00:25:21,620 --> 00:25:24,720
It had to be laboriously rewired each time it was programmed

319
00:25:24,721 --> 00:25:27,940
and couldn't make logical decisions based on its calculations.

320
00:25:28,940 --> 00:25:31,980
But with tremendous expenditures of time and money,

321
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:35,340
ENIAC had proved that computers could be constructed.

322
00:25:35,341 --> 00:25:41,600
However, except for arcane scientific calculation, did anyone really want them?

323
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:47,780
The question lingered, could anyone build a really practical computer?

324
00:25:47,781 --> 00:26:01,781
Just before the end of the Second World War, an advisor to the ENIAC project, John von Neumann,

325
00:26:03,160 --> 00:26:07,040
wrote a paper that was to greatly affect the next stage of computer design.

326
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:12,960
Von Neumann possessed a photographic memory, an incomparably fast mind,

327
00:26:12,961 --> 00:26:16,720
and was one of the principal scientists involved in the Manhattan Project,

328
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:18,680
the building of the atomic bomb.

329
00:26:19,260 --> 00:26:21,320
He was also an advisor on ENIAC.

330
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:26,860
The paper von Neumann wrote after the war delineated the structure of a modern computer.

331
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:30,880
The paper drew heavily on the work building the ENIAC,

332
00:26:31,320 --> 00:26:34,620
yet was undeniably augmented by von Neumann's brilliance.

333
00:26:35,960 --> 00:26:39,060
Von Neumann's computer was to have a processing unit,

334
00:26:39,380 --> 00:26:42,600
a controlling unit, memory, input, and output.

335
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:49,440
But most importantly in the evolution of computers, it would hold its programming internally in its memory.

336
00:26:51,420 --> 00:26:55,060
Internally held programs give computers their power and versatility,

337
00:26:55,460 --> 00:27:01,520
because an internal program can modify what it does based on data or the results of computations.

338
00:27:02,540 --> 00:27:09,580
In machines like the ENIAC, programming had been hardwired or fixed, so the machine was much less adaptable.

339
00:27:10,540 --> 00:27:16,140
The idea for storing the program internally was the last key to developing the true computer.

340
00:27:16,740 --> 00:27:20,580
But whether it was von Neumann's idea has long been hotly debated.

341
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:27,240
Eckert and Mockley claimed they formulated internal programs as a natural part of their work building ENIAC,

342
00:27:27,241 --> 00:27:30,780
although they couldn't stop and incorporate the idea into the machine.

343
00:27:31,620 --> 00:27:36,680
But many who read the paper assumed that all the genius behind it was from the great von Neumann.

344
00:27:36,681 --> 00:27:41,680
He was one of the most widely regarded mathematicians in the world.

345
00:27:42,540 --> 00:27:44,860
Eckerd and Mockley were relatively obscure.

346
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:48,700
Eckerd was a young man just out of school.

347
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,860
Mockley had been a professor at a fairly out-of-the-way college.

348
00:27:53,400 --> 00:27:55,980
They didn't have international reputations.

349
00:27:57,040 --> 00:28:00,220
Eckerd and Mockley weren't included as authors in the paper.

350
00:28:00,520 --> 00:28:02,060
They felt they had been betrayed.

351
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:07,400
The most important effect of von Neumann's paper was to spur computer development.

352
00:28:08,180 --> 00:28:14,600
Eckert and Mockley moved into offices in Philadelphia, hired a staff, and set up a company that would build a business computer.

353
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:16,780
They called it the UNIVAC.

354
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:25,100
They signed a fixed-cost contract to build a UNIVAC for the Census Bureau, rolled up their sleeves, and went to work.

355
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:30,940
Unfortunately, building UNIVAC turned out to be a monumental undertaking.

356
00:28:30,941 --> 00:28:39,320
As they struggled to make UNIVAC real, they also struggled financially and needed to be bailed out by a series of larger companies.

357
00:28:40,320 --> 00:28:45,300
They eventually joined forces with Remington Rand, a flourishing typewriter manufacturer.

358
00:28:47,080 --> 00:28:54,780
In March of 1951, after six years of toil, they finally delivered the first UNIVAC to the Census Bureau.

359
00:28:54,781 --> 00:29:01,780
Unlike ENIAC, the UNIVAC was an entire computer system designed for business.

360
00:29:01,781 --> 00:29:06,780
UNIVAC could be programmed for a variety of data processing tasks.

361
00:29:06,781 --> 00:29:11,780
Compact tape drives held data and results were automatically printed.

362
00:29:11,781 --> 00:29:16,780
But even with the backing of Remington Rand, sales remained slow.

363
00:29:16,781 --> 00:29:21,780
Very few people understood how useful a computer could be.

364
00:29:24,780 --> 00:29:31,780
dramatically one night in 1952.

365
00:29:32,540 --> 00:29:36,900
In a brilliant public relations move, Remington Rand arranged with CBS to use a

366
00:29:36,901 --> 00:29:40,860
UNIVAC on election night to predict the outcome of the presidential race

367
00:29:40,861 --> 00:29:46,160
between Dwight Eisenhower and Adlai Stevenson. Polls said the race was too

368
00:29:46,161 --> 00:29:53,160
close to call. No one had ever programmed a computer to make electoral predictions.

369
00:29:53,161 --> 00:29:57,900
Eckert and Mockley's engineers entered their customized algorithms right until airtime.

370
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:04,803
The operators fed in the results of selected eastern precincts and at 9 p.m.

371
00:30:04,804 --> 00:30:05,940
ran their program.

372
00:30:07,320 --> 00:30:11,580
UNIVAC predicted a landslide for Eisenhower, but the polls said differently.

373
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:17,240
The operators didn't believe what UNIVAC was telling them and assumed their programming was at fault.

374
00:30:18,460 --> 00:30:22,240
Quickly, they reprogrammed the machine to better reflect what the experts predicted.

375
00:30:22,241 --> 00:30:29,240
As the night went on, it became clear that Eisenhower would indeed win by a landslide.

376
00:30:29,241 --> 00:30:34,240
CBS sheepishly announced that they hadn't believed the machines.

377
00:30:34,241 --> 00:30:40,240
When all the votes were tallied, Univac's initial prediction was off by less than 1% of the final result,

378
00:30:40,241 --> 00:30:45,240
an extraordinary prediction even by today's standards.

379
00:30:45,241 --> 00:30:50,240
The power and utility of the computer had been proved.

380
00:30:52,240 --> 00:31:01,100
After the success of UNIVAC, various companies began to see a future in computer development.

381
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:08,040
New companies like Burroughs, as well as old giants like General Electric, jumped into the computer business.

382
00:31:09,800 --> 00:31:16,240
But most large American businesses were dependent on the data processing systems provided by one company,

383
00:31:17,060 --> 00:31:19,560
the office machine monolith IBM.

384
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:25,240
IBM had no computers.

385
00:31:25,241 --> 00:31:31,240
IBM's aging leader, the legendary sales guru Thomas Watson, Sr.,

386
00:31:31,241 --> 00:31:36,240
was not eager to jump into the enormously costly development of computers.

387
00:31:36,241 --> 00:31:40,240
A computer back then contained thousands of vacuum tubes,

388
00:31:40,241 --> 00:31:44,240
occupied one or more large rooms,

389
00:31:44,241 --> 00:31:48,240
and required a small army of attendants to run.

390
00:31:48,241 --> 00:31:48,941
to run.

391
00:31:48,942 --> 00:31:54,720
IBM and most other people didn't see how computers could be used in business.

392
00:31:55,520 --> 00:32:01,474
It took Watson's son, Tom Watson Jr., to lead IBM into the computer age.

393
00:32:01,475 --> 00:32:02,880
As Watson himself

394
00:32:02,881 --> 00:32:08,340
recalls, the move was spurred by IBM's customers who were fed up with bulky punch cards.

395
00:32:08,900 --> 00:32:13,960
I remember particularly Jim Madden, then vice president of the Metropolitan Life,

396
00:32:13,961 --> 00:32:18,960
who said, we're going to cancel our IBM just the minute we learned to do this in tapes

397
00:32:18,961 --> 00:32:21,800
because three floors of the Metropolitan Life building

398
00:32:21,801 --> 00:32:25,520
are used to store the cards of our customers' accounts.

399
00:32:25,760 --> 00:32:29,560
And if we keep going the way we will, they'll occupy the whole building.

400
00:32:29,980 --> 00:32:32,280
We were threatened into this progress.

401
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:35,700
Faced with the prospect of losing customers,

402
00:32:35,880 --> 00:32:38,740
Tom Watson Jr. ordered the development of a computer.

403
00:32:38,741 --> 00:32:44,620
IBM's famous sales force told their customers a computer would arrive soon.

404
00:32:45,220 --> 00:32:47,080
In fact, it was still being planned.

405
00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:52,300
Finally, in 1953, IBM unveiled the 701.

406
00:32:53,480 --> 00:32:56,140
Although technologically inferior to the UNIVAC,

407
00:32:56,440 --> 00:33:00,540
the 701 and other early IBM computers were hits with the customers

408
00:33:00,541 --> 00:33:04,380
because they conformed to standard IBM systems and support.

409
00:33:04,381 --> 00:33:09,380
IBM saw the future and it was computers.

410
00:33:09,381 --> 00:33:13,380
They redirected corporate efforts to computer development.

411
00:33:13,381 --> 00:33:17,380
IBM's efforts paid off and by the beginning of the 1960s,

412
00:33:17,381 --> 00:33:21,380
the company's large mainframe computers dominated the business.

413
00:33:21,381 --> 00:33:27,380
But the development of the computer was about to go in an altogether different direction.

414
00:33:34,380 --> 00:33:48,380
We choose to go to the moon in this decay and do the other things, not because they are easy, but because they are hard.

415
00:33:51,900 --> 00:33:56,400
In 1961, America was trailing Russia in the race for space.

416
00:33:56,401 --> 00:34:07,640
1961, a year of achievement for Soviet scientists in the race for space.

417
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:14,520
Yuri Gagarin has become the first human to orbit the Earth,

418
00:34:15,140 --> 00:34:19,400
and crowds in Moscow's Red Square salute the 27-year-old cosmonaut.

419
00:34:20,160 --> 00:34:22,920
As NASA engineers began planning the lunar mission,

420
00:34:22,921 --> 00:34:28,840
they realized a computer as powerful as one currently the size of a room must be on board.

421
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:33,840
The engineers wondered, is such a small computer even possible?

422
00:34:34,680 --> 00:34:38,340
The first great breakthrough that would lead to computer miniaturization

423
00:34:38,341 --> 00:34:41,740
had already been made on December 23, 1947,

424
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:44,600
when three scientists at Bell Labs,

425
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:48,720
William Shockley, Walter Brethain, and John Bardeen,

426
00:34:49,020 --> 00:34:50,200
invented the transistor.

427
00:34:51,140 --> 00:34:56,880
Formed on the semiconductor silicon, the transistor could replace large vacuum tubes in computers.

428
00:34:58,300 --> 00:35:04,040
Compared to vacuum tubes, transistors were tiny, required little power, and produced little heat.

429
00:35:04,980 --> 00:35:11,160
The breakthrough was sufficiently important that the three inventors of the transistor were awarded the Nobel Prize.

430
00:35:12,480 --> 00:35:17,100
A computer that could navigate to the moon and back would require thousands of transistors,

431
00:35:17,280 --> 00:35:19,980
and although small, they were not nearly small enough.

432
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:31,328
The next step in miniaturization occurred in 1959 when Robert Noyce and Jack Kilby, engineers for rival transistor manufacturers, independently came up with

433
00:35:31,329 --> 00:35:35,180
breakthroughs that led to the same revolutionary idea.

434
00:35:35,181 --> 00:35:45,440
An entire network of electronic components, transistors, diodes, capacitors, and resistors could be incorporated onto a single chip of silicone.

435
00:35:45,441 --> 00:35:51,440
This great innovation in electronics was called the integrated circuit.

436
00:35:51,441 --> 00:35:59,440
Using integrated circuits, a 10-ounce computer was built that was as powerful as a 30-pound one made of transistors.

437
00:35:59,441 --> 00:36:04,440
But integrated circuits had an inherent problem.

438
00:36:04,441 --> 00:36:08,440
They were difficult to manufacture and therefore were expensive.

439
00:36:08,441 --> 00:36:12,440
But the space race had started just in time to pay for them.

440
00:36:12,441 --> 00:36:20,920
In 1969, 5,000 integrated circuits made up the heart of each of two identical computers,

441
00:36:21,460 --> 00:36:24,720
one on the lunar orbiter and one on the lander.

442
00:36:25,640 --> 00:36:31,840
For their size, these were the most powerful computers on Earth, soon to leave the Earth entirely.

443
00:36:33,680 --> 00:36:35,080
Beautiful, just beautiful.

444
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:39,840
I'm going to step off the land mount.

445
00:36:39,841 --> 00:36:44,840
As Neil Armstrong took one small step in the lunar dust,

446
00:36:44,841 --> 00:36:49,840
Intel engineer Ted Hoff was making the last great leap in miniaturization,

447
00:36:49,841 --> 00:36:54,840
developing an idea that would put an entire computer on a chip of silicone,

448
00:36:54,841 --> 00:36:56,840
the microprocessor.

449
00:36:56,841 --> 00:37:01,840
The genie would be out of its bottle, beginning the computer revolution,

450
00:37:01,841 --> 00:37:04,840
and changing the world forever.

451
00:37:21,814 --> 00:37:30,660
Apollo 13, intended as the third lunar landing, had just lost two fuel cells and was venting oxygen into space, 200,000 miles from Earth.

452
00:37:30,661 --> 00:37:35,620
Soon, the second oxygen tank would begin losing pressure.

453
00:37:36,360 --> 00:37:45,380
The astronauts would die, unless they could precisely align their spacecraft and fire their rocket to slingshot themselves around the moon and back to Earth.

454
00:37:46,340 --> 00:37:49,980
Critical to the alignment was Apollo's state-of-the-art guidance computer.

455
00:37:50,980 --> 00:37:52,320
A new trajectory was figured.

456
00:37:52,720 --> 00:37:54,940
The all-or-nothing rocket firing was made.

457
00:37:54,941 --> 00:38:01,700
The Apollo 13 crew miraculously returned to Earth with the help of their small and powerful computer.

458
00:38:02,720 --> 00:38:05,320
By the last Apollo mission, two and one-half years later,

459
00:38:05,640 --> 00:38:09,400
computers as powerful as those on Apollo would be available to everyone.

460
00:38:10,160 --> 00:38:13,020
That was because soon before the Apollo 13 flight,

461
00:38:13,360 --> 00:38:17,460
an engineer at Intel, Ted Hoff, had come up with an ingenious idea.

462
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:23,240
Hoff had been told to design 12 separate integrated circuits to make a Japanese pocket calculator.

463
00:38:23,241 --> 00:38:29,320
He suggested placing the entire processing unit on a chip and programming it just like a computer.

464
00:38:30,320 --> 00:38:35,780
Intel developed the idea, and by 1970, they had a working model of a microprocessor.

465
00:38:37,160 --> 00:38:44,540
It was the invention not just of integrated circuits, but of a particular kind of integrated circuit,

466
00:38:44,660 --> 00:38:48,680
the microprocessor that makes today's personal computers possible.

467
00:38:48,681 --> 00:38:54,680
Smaller than a fingernail, a microprocessor contains many of the components of a computer,

468
00:38:54,681 --> 00:39:01,680
including a control unit, a clock, and areas where data can be stored and modified.

469
00:39:01,681 --> 00:39:07,680
Processing power was about to become very cheap and very compact.

470
00:39:07,681 --> 00:39:12,680
In the mid-1970s, two friends, Stephen Wozniak and Steve Jobs,

471
00:39:12,681 --> 00:39:17,680
were manufacturing a small computer in a Palo Alto garage.

472
00:39:17,681 --> 00:39:20,340
Steve Jobs was a college dropout,

473
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:23,260
but he was a college dropout with a difference.

474
00:39:23,360 --> 00:39:24,400
He was very intelligent.

475
00:39:25,200 --> 00:39:26,860
He had a lot of street smarts.

476
00:39:27,200 --> 00:39:28,720
He was also extremely ambitious.

477
00:39:29,620 --> 00:39:31,300
I think perhaps more important than that,

478
00:39:31,301 --> 00:39:34,300
he knew the other Steve, Steve Wozniak,

479
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:36,980
had created something exceptional.

480
00:39:38,160 --> 00:39:39,880
Steve Jobs sold his Volkswagen,

481
00:39:40,340 --> 00:39:42,720
and Steven Wozniak sold his HP calculator

482
00:39:42,721 --> 00:39:43,940
to finance their company

483
00:39:43,941 --> 00:39:46,600
that would revolutionize the computer industry.

484
00:39:47,520 --> 00:39:52,500
Jobs trekked all over the San Francisco Bay Area to find buyers for the $500 machine,

485
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:54,540
which they called the Apple I.

486
00:39:55,560 --> 00:39:58,520
The Apple I was large and unwieldy.

487
00:39:58,960 --> 00:40:03,460
Jobs realized they needed a new design for a computer that anyone could use.

488
00:40:03,940 --> 00:40:06,140
Wozniak began to build the Apple II.

489
00:40:06,960 --> 00:40:11,440
The fate of Apple changed dramatically when, in the fall of 1976,

490
00:40:12,060 --> 00:40:15,800
a visitor to Wozniak's garage saw the prototype of the Apple II.

491
00:40:16,600 --> 00:40:21,440
The visitor was Mike Markola, who at 32 had retired from Intel as a millionaire.

492
00:40:22,080 --> 00:40:27,200
He was so impressed by the Apple II that he joined Apple and put it on a sound business footing.

493
00:40:28,040 --> 00:40:31,660
The Apple II was introduced to the public and sales skyrocketed.

494
00:40:31,661 --> 00:40:37,860
But in 1978, even with the success of the Apple II, using a computer wasn't easy.

495
00:40:37,861 --> 00:40:47,260
The early Apple computers, as well as all early personal computers, did not have graphical interfaces.

496
00:40:47,820 --> 00:40:48,940
They didn't have mice.

497
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:55,160
They had what's known as a command line interface.

498
00:40:55,620 --> 00:41:01,840
That is, you typed instructions into the computer and your instructions appeared as text on the screen.

499
00:41:01,841 --> 00:41:11,620
A simple to use computer had been conceived by a computer scientist named Doug Engelbart.

500
00:41:11,621 --> 00:41:24,600
He demonstrated his vision in 1968 at the Fall Joint Computer Conference in San Francisco.

501
00:41:24,601 --> 00:41:29,220
Wielding a keyboard and a pointing device he called a mouse, Engelbart worked with a

502
00:41:29,221 --> 00:41:35,620
computer 30 miles away linked by microwaves and demonstrated word processing and hypertext.

503
00:41:36,980 --> 00:41:42,660
Many in the audience went home inspired, but one group alone was to fulfill Engelbart's vision.

504
00:41:44,100 --> 00:41:48,020
That group was just down the road from San Francisco at Xerox PARC.

505
00:41:49,620 --> 00:41:55,780
In 1970, Xerox dominated the copier industry but thought the future might be in computers.

506
00:41:55,781 --> 00:42:02,040
A young Xerox executive, Robert Taylor, worked with a team to transform the way the computer

507
00:42:02,041 --> 00:42:03,960
industry was perceived.

508
00:42:03,961 --> 00:42:06,100
As Robert Taylor himself remembers...

509
00:42:06,101 --> 00:42:14,540
The chairman of Xerox at the time, Peter McCullough, made a speech where he said that Xerox was

510
00:42:14,541 --> 00:42:18,080
going to become the architecture of information.

511
00:42:18,081 --> 00:42:23,820
So I asked his speechwriter not too long after that, what did that mean?

512
00:42:23,821 --> 00:42:29,260
I had an idea about what it ought to mean and so did some other people and the speech

513
00:42:29,261 --> 00:42:34,140
writer said well he didn't really know but it's a ringing phrase and so I said well we're

514
00:42:34,141 --> 00:42:36,900
going to make it happen.

515
00:42:36,901 --> 00:42:41,160
Robert Taylor hired many of the country's top computer scientists and challenged them

516
00:42:41,161 --> 00:42:45,780
to create an easy to use personal computer.

517
00:42:45,781 --> 00:42:50,220
The result was the Alto which incorporated many of the innovations in personal computers

518
00:42:50,221 --> 00:42:54,060
we take for granted today, all developed at Xerox PARC.

519
00:42:55,360 --> 00:42:58,220
The Alto used a mouse, a graphical interface,

520
00:42:58,560 --> 00:43:01,060
built-in networking, and printed on a laser printer.

521
00:43:02,040 --> 00:43:05,500
Xerox developed the star, the commercial model of the Alto,

522
00:43:05,900 --> 00:43:07,160
but it never sold well.

523
00:43:08,160 --> 00:43:14,500
Xerox was a large and very successful photocopier company,

524
00:43:15,060 --> 00:43:18,140
and it didn't really understand computers.

525
00:43:18,141 --> 00:43:23,420
didn't appreciate the brilliance, the originality,

526
00:43:24,120 --> 00:43:32,160
and the enormous commercial worth of the computer developments at Xerox Park.

527
00:43:32,460 --> 00:43:34,280
But Steve Jobs did.

528
00:43:41,240 --> 00:43:46,740
In 1979, Steve Jobs visited Xerox Park and saw the Alto.

529
00:43:46,741 --> 00:43:52,380
He returned to Apple and immediately set to work on what would become the Macintosh computer,

530
00:43:52,700 --> 00:43:56,400
the first popular personal computer similar to those used today.

531
00:43:57,180 --> 00:44:03,240
What made the Macintosh easy to use was its operating system and applications, otherwise known as software.

532
00:44:04,240 --> 00:44:07,940
Increasingly, software was dominating the advances made in computers.

533
00:44:08,680 --> 00:44:12,680
Bill Gates, the young president of computing software company Microsoft,

534
00:44:12,900 --> 00:44:16,480
understood the importance of software to the future of computers.

535
00:44:16,740 --> 00:44:22,620
and parlayed this vision into a vast software empire, making him the richest man in the world.

536
00:44:23,460 --> 00:44:27,960
The dream of a machine that could think had come from a mechanical device to an electronic one,

537
00:44:28,240 --> 00:44:34,780
where the bits of coded information that ran it were as important, perhaps more important, than the machine that they controlled.

538
00:44:35,860 --> 00:44:41,900
This may have been the best evidence that a thinking machine had arrived and could now be placed on your desk.

539
00:44:41,901 --> 00:44:46,900
Soon, nearly half the jobs in America would use the computer.

540
00:44:46,901 --> 00:44:57,992
Within the decade, microprocessors would be everywhere, incorporated into automobiles, appliances, and scientific instruments, significantly increasing their

541
00:44:57,993 --> 00:44:59,900
capability and reliability.

542
00:44:59,901 --> 00:45:07,900
Unprecedented fortunes would be made in businesses that hadn't existed a decade before.

543
00:45:07,901 --> 00:45:16,060
Education would be transformed forever as the access to information would be transferred from libraries and universities to your desktop.

544
00:45:16,920 --> 00:45:25,460
The earth would shrink with unprecedented speed as a worldwide communication grid became accessible to anyone with a computer and modem.

545
00:45:27,380 --> 00:45:31,640
And even now, the evolution of a thinking machine isn't finished.

546
00:45:32,360 --> 00:45:35,140
Computers have already changed the way we live.

547
00:45:35,141 --> 00:45:40,760
and they'll change the way we explore our world and other worlds in the 21st century.

548
00:45:41,720 --> 00:45:46,560
They will take us to distant galaxies and they will connect us right here on Earth.

549
00:45:47,520 --> 00:45:50,560
Computers will continue to become more interconnected.

550
00:45:51,560 --> 00:45:54,860
They will continue to become smaller, faster, cheaper.

551
00:45:56,140 --> 00:45:58,600
And software will become more powerful.

552
00:45:58,601 --> 00:46:07,080
Minds that have yet to be formed will mold this power to create new marvels inconceivable to us today

